INT. TARA'S BEDROOM - DAY

TARA (8) sits on her neatly made bed. She wears striped socks and a bright lilac raincoat.

Tara has generalized anxiety disorder. Today, her chest feels tight. Her heart beats a steady, rapid rhythm.

Tara opens a green scrapbook on her lap. The pages are filled with pressed leaves, each labeled with neat handwriting. The final page is empty. It has a label: "Golden Birch Leaf."

TARA
I will find you today.

Tara zips up her small backpack. She checks the straps. She pulls them until they are perfectly snug.

On her desk sits her weather-house toy. Inside the little wooden house, two figures sit on a track. The tiny wooden lady is inside. The tiny wooden man with the blue umbrella is starting to slide out.

Tara taps the wooden roof. The man slides a millimeter further.

DEV (11) stands in the doorway. He wears a light t-shirt and shorts. He holds a plastic water bottle.

DEV
Are you ready, Tara? Mom says we have to stay on the main trail.

TARA
I am ready. I have my map. I have my flashlight. I have my whistle.

DEV
It is eighty degrees outside. Why are you wearing a raincoat?

TARA
My body says I need it.

DEV
You always worry too much. Let us go before the sun gets too hot.

EXT. FORESTRY TRAIL - DAY

Tara and Dev walk along a dirt path. Tall birch and oak trees line the trail. Sunlight filters through the green canopy.

Tara walks with her head down. She scans the forest floor. She is looking for her golden birch leaf.

She stops to examine a yellow leaf. She shakes her head. She leaves it on the ground.

DEV
We have been walking for an hour. Can we go back soon?

TARA
Not yet. The golden birch trees are just ahead near the ridge.

Tara stops. She stands very still. She touches her chest.

Her generalized anxiety disorder makes her highly sensitive to changes in her environment. Right now, her skin feels cold. The air feels heavy.

EXT. OAK GROVE - DAY

The wind has stopped completely. The leaves on the trees do not move. The birds have stopped singing.

Tara looks up at the sky. The light has changed from bright yellow to a pale, flat grey.

TARA
Dev. Look at the leaves on the silver maples.

DEV
They are just leaves, Tara.

TARA
They are turned upside down. They do that when the humidity rises fast. A storm is coming.

Dev pulls out his phone. He taps the screen.

DEV
The weather app says zero percent chance of rain. It is totally clear.

TARA
The app is looking at the sky from an hour ago. My body is looking at the sky right now.

Tara reaches into her backpack. She pulls out the weather-house toy. She holds it flat on her palm.

The tiny wooden man with the blue umbrella is completely outside the house. The tiny wooden lady is hidden deep inside.

TARA
See? The air is heavy. The toy knows.

DEV
That is just a toy, Tara. Come on.

SAM (9) runs down the trail from the opposite direction. He carries a wooden walking stick.

SAM
Hey! You guys should come up to the high ridge. You can see the whole valley from up there!

TARA
No, Sam. Do not go to the ridge. It is too open. A storm is coming.

SAM
There are no clouds, Tara. You are always so scared of everything.

TARA
I am not scared. I am paying attention.

Tara points toward the western horizon. Behind the tall pines of the ridge, a dark, bruised purple wall of clouds is rising fast.

EXT. RIDGE PATH - DAY

A sudden, cold gust of wind rushes through the trees. It whips Tara's short bunches of hair across her face.

Dev's t-shirt flaps wildly. He shivers.

DEV
Wow. That got cold fast.

He looks at his phone. The screen shows a spinning loading icon.

DEV
No signal. Wait, the screen just updated. Severe storm warning!

SAM
Oh no! The trailhead is a forty-minute walk back!

The wind howls louder. The trees begin to bend.

TARA
We cannot make it to the trailhead. We have to go to the stone shelter.

SAM
Where is that?

TARA
It is two minutes from here. Follow me.

Tara takes charge. She leads the way down a side path. Her heart is pounding against her ribs. Her chest is tight, but her steps are steady and sure.

EXT. STONE SHELTER PATH - DAY

The sky is now almost completely black. The wind tears leaves from the branches.

Tara pulls her flashlight from her backpack. She turns it on, casting a bright beam on the rocky path.

TARA
Watch the wet roots! They are slippery!

Dev slips on a wet patch of mud. Tara catches his arm, steadying him.

DEV
Thanks, Tara.

TARA
Keep moving. Almost there.

First one, then dozens of giant, heavy raindrops slam into the dirt floor.

Tara spots the small stone cabin ahead. It has a thick wooden door.

TARA
Run!

They sprint the final ten yards as the sky opens up. A sheet of water pours down from the clouds.

INT. STONE SHELTER - CONTINUOUS

Tara pushes the heavy wooden door open. Dev and Sam scramble inside. Tara follows and slams the door shut behind them.

The sound of the rain outside is deafening, drumming against the stone roof. Thunder rumbles in the distance, shaking the walls.

Sam sits on the wooden bench, catching his breath. He looks at Tara with wide eyes.

SAM
You knew exactly what to do. How did you know the storm was coming so fast?

TARA
My body told me. It is like an early-warning system. I just had to listen to it.

DEV
I am sorry I did not believe you, Tara. You saved us from getting soaked on the ridge.

TARA
That is okay. We are safe now.

Tara sits on the stone floor. She keeps her lilac raincoat zipped. Her chest still feels tight. Her anxiety is still there, humming quietly inside her. She takes a deep breath and lets it out slowly.

She looks down at the dry stone floor near the corner of the shelter.

Sitting in a dry patch of dust is a single, perfect leaf. It is a brilliant, glowing golden-yellow with delicate serrated edges.

Tara gasps. She reaches out and gently picks it up.

TARA
A golden birch leaf.

She opens her scrapbook to the final page. She carefully places the leaf under the protective plastic sheet.

She smiles. Her heart is still beating fast, but she is exactly where she needs to be.

DEV
Did you find what you wanted?

TARA
Yes. I found it.